{
	title: 'hal/intro: a friendly first-run guide',
	spec: `hal/intro (src/host/synthetic.ts) is the first-run guide: a synthetic model that asks with forms and acts on the answers, with no provider needed. It is short because the user is eager to start. Every run asks the same questions; rerunning it updates the profile instead of skipping known fields. The flow, in order:

1) Greeting, exactly: "Hello — I am HAL 9001, your personal agent harness. You can call me Hal." Then, as a separate message after a short pause, the number of questions this run will ask, for example "I have three questions for you." Name, language and model are the questions; the Serper question adds one only when no Serper key exists. Login and timezone confirmation are not counted. Apart from this greeting and the Dave placeholder there are no HAL 9000 references.
2) "What should I call you? (Optional)" as a text field whose placeholder is Dave. The placeholder is never saved.
3) "What's your preferred language (e.g. US English)?"
4) Timezone, never typed as an IANA identifier by the user. It uses the client-reported timezone (task wq). When the client and host zones are equal and not UTC-like, save it without asking. When they differ, or both are UTC-like, ask "It seems like you are in the <city> timezone (<zone>). Correct?" as Yes/No. No offers a choice of this device's zone, the host's zone, or Other. Other takes a city or zone name, matched case-insensitively against Intl.supportedValuesOf('timeZone'), including by the city part, with a friendly retry when nothing matches. With no client signal, offer the host zone for confirmation unless it is UTC-like; otherwise leave the timezone unchanged.
5) Right after these steps write USER.md (task ky) and say: "I saved your answers to <absolute path of USER.md>. You can edit it to update your personal preferences." Saving comes before login so a failed or abandoned login cannot lose the answers.
6) Login, only when no provider has a usable credential: a nonempty file account (accessToken or apiKey) or an environment API key. It explains that /login signs in with Claude or ChatGPT and that API keys work too, offers to start /login claude, /login chatgpt or /login opencode, or Skip, and continues the intro after login.
7) Model: "Which model should Hal use?" as a choice labelled with human display names, never raw IDs. It offers the current targets of the claude and gpt aliases, resolved through the model registry rather than hard-coded, each only when its provider has a credential, plus Other. Choosing a model sets the default for new sessions and switches this tab to it. Other explains that /model or Ctrl-M picks any model. With no credentials at all, skip this step.
8) Serper web search setup as before: explain the google tool through Serper, ask Yes/No, take the key in a secret field and store it through apiKeys.save; skipped when a key exists.
9) Close with three or four short tips (Escape pauses, Alt-Enter queues, /help, /keys, the web client address) and which model this tab now uses.

Pacing: consecutive intro messages arrive with short human-like pauses of a few hundred milliseconds. Pauses never block typing, scrolling or answering, and the user is never left without a way to answer or type. If the user submits text while the intro has no pending question, reply: I see you typed: "<text>" — but I'm not a language model, so I can't understand you. Continue with the intro? (Yes/No). Yes continues and No ends the intro, keeping what was saved.

It is resumable: after a restart or a steered message it continues from the first step of the current run that has no answer record, never asking twice within a run. A fresh home with no sessions opens its first tab on hal/intro. Tests cover the step order with skips, resume mid-run, no credentials offering /login, the model options following credentials and aliases, and the timezone decisions.`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'hal/intro advances by named answer records and USER.md rather than a volatile step counter. The first tab alone starts on hal/intro; provider tests with preexisting homes must seed a provider session. Login launches the real slash command after the intro turn; Serper uses apiKeys.save/liveFile with 0600 and its secret answer never enters history.',
		'E2E provider tests must wait for the preexisting session’s first host socket and then the peer UI before sending commands; session count is nonzero before either process starts, so using it as readiness races and drops keystrokes.',
		'Keep model choices tied to the available credential provider IDs, not the configured default; an unconfigured default must not sneak into the options. If the login step is skipped with no credentials, go straight to search.'
	],
	needs: ['n2', 'wq']
}
